INT. PARKING LOT - MERIDIAN SYNERGIES - FIRE DRILL - DAY

Two hundred employees in lanyards stand on hot asphalt in neat clusters. A sign reads ASSEMBLY POINT B.

GWENDOLYN PRUITT (50s, VP of Operations, clipboard, sunglasses, an air of command) stands at the center, tapping a pen. HAL BORSTEIN (40s, sales director, shirt-sleeves rolled, a stress ball in each fist) squeezes both. TILLY NAKAMURA (30s, HR, lanyard heavy with badges) counts heads and gets a different number every time. MORTIMER GAULT (60s, CFO, a bow tie, a thermos of something) sits on a curb as though he'd been assigned one. YVETTE LAROCHE (30s, marketing lead, phone out, thumbs flying) isn't present in any way that matters.

At the edge, an INTERN (20s, wrinkled polo, a name tag that says HELLO MY NAME IS and nothing else) stands holding a box of donuts.

GWENDOLYN
Tilly, status.

TILLY
Two hundred and four. Or two hundred and six.

GWENDOLYN
We have two hundred and one employees.

TILLY
Then I have five ghosts.

HAL
That's synergy.

YVETTE
(not looking up)
Can you all not.

GWENDOLYN
Yvette, put down the phone.

YVETTE
It's a work phone.

GWENDOLYN
It's a fire drill.

YVETTE
It's a very productive fire drill.

MORTIMER
(from the curb)
In my day, we ran out of the building screaming and called it Tuesday.

GWENDOLYN
Mortimer, that's not helpful.

MORTIMER
It wasn't meant to be.

The Intern lifts the donut box.

INTERN
Does anyone want a donut?

Everyone turns.

GWENDOLYN
Who authorized donuts?

INTERN
Nobody. I thought it would be nice.

GWENDOLYN
Who are you?

INTERN
The, um. Intern?

GWENDOLYN
What's your name?

INTERN
Colton.

TILLY
(consulting her clipboard)
There's no Colton on the roster.

COLTON
I'm new.

HAL
(dual with Tilly)
Nobody told us there was a new guy.

TILLY
^There is no Colton, Gwendolyn. I checked all three lists.

GWENDOLYN
Colton, who hired you?

COLTON
A man in the elevator.

MORTIMER
That'll be me.

Silence. Mortimer sips from his thermos, entirely at peace.

GWENDOLYN
Mortimer, you can't hire a person in an elevator.

MORTIMER
The elevator was stuck. It was either hire him or listen to him hum.

COLTON
I wasn't humming.

MORTIMER
You were humming "Delilah."

HAL
You can hire a guy because he hums "Delilah"?

MORTIMER
He hummed it in the right key.

YVETTE
(looking up, finally)
Wait. Are we talking about the fire drill or about Colton?

GWENDOLYN
We are talking about neither. We are talking about a protocol.

YVETTE
What protocol?

GWENDOLYN
There is a protocol, and it states that employees assemble at the point, and wait for the all clear.

HAL
Gwen, how long have we been waiting?

GWENDOLYN
Forty minutes.

HAL
Who's giving the all clear?

Everyone looks at the building. Smoke, thin and gray, curls from a third-floor window.

YVETTE
(very quietly, to herself)
Oh, that's a real one.

They all turn together to look at the window. Colton sets down the donuts.

COLTON
Should I call someone?

GWENDOLYN
(brightly)
No, Colton. I think we've got this under control.

Behind her, a window pops and glass rains on the asphalt.

TILLY
Two hundred and one.

GWENDOLYN
Thank you, Tilly.

TILLY
And Colton.

GWENDOLYN
Two hundred and two.

HAL
(squeezing both stress balls)
I'd like it noted that I said we should have had donuts.
